<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popocatépetl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Popocatépetl is an active volcano in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, at about 5,400 metres the country's second-highest peak, on the borders of the states of Puebla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> and Mexico. Its name means 'smoking mountain' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>. Eruptions in antiquity displaced communities in the Puebla–Tlaxcala region, and it has been frequently active since 1994, threatening the millions who live nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/popocatepetl.jpg" alt="Popocatépetl" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakub Hejtmánek · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mexico-Popocatepetl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Volcanic activity helped to shift this balance: eruptions of <b>Popocatépetl</b>, probably in the first century CE, displaced communities to the east, and the eruption of Xitle buried Cuicuilco under lava, although the date of that eruption is debated and may be considerably later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Countless place names in Mexico and Central America are Nahuatl: Mexico itself, Guatemala, Acapulco, Oaxaca and the volcano <b>Popocatépetl</b>, “smoking mountain”.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Chalco developed around the eastern shore of Lake Chalco and the slopes of the volcanoes <b>Popocatépetl</b> and Iztaccíhuatl.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalco">Chalco</a></li></ul></section>
